<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denizli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ladik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denizli is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, near the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laodicea-on-the-lycus">Laodicea on the Lycus</a>. The medieval Turkish town, known as Ladik, grew up nearby after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> groups took the area in the late 13th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited it around 1333, praising its gold-embroidered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth. Today Denizli is a provincial capital and a centre of the Turkish textile industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denizli.jpg" alt="Denizli" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karelj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pamukale_Turkey_2013_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Denizli lies in Aegean Turkey and is the seat of Denizli Province. It stands near the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laodicea-on-the-lycus">Laodicea on the Lycus</a>, the ancient metropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, and of Hierapolis, with the mineral-coated hillside hot springs of Pamukkale about 20 kilometres to the north. The ruins of Colossae lie near Honaz, about 16 kilometres to the west. The climate is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, with hot, dry summers and mild to cool, rainy winters.</p><p>The word deniz means sea or large lake, though none lies nearby. The original Turkish name is said to have been Tonguzlu, from a word for wild boar, meaning a place where boars dwell. Sound changes turned it into denizlü and then Denizli, which avoided the association with the pig, considered offensive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Ibn Battuta recorded the city as Dun Ghuzluh, said to mean the town of the swine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In antiquity an important Greek town called Attouda stood here, through the Greek and Roman eras and into the Byzantine period, near Hierapolis and Laodicea. Seljuk Turks conquered the city, and inhabitants of Laodicea were resettled here in the Seljuk period.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited, noted seven mosques for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a>, splendid gardens, perennial streams and gushing springs. He also noted that most of the artisans were Greek women, because many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> lived there subject to the Turks and paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> and other taxes to the sultan. He added that he needed an escort of horsemen on the way, since the surrounding plain was infested by a band of brigands called al-Jarmiyān, a reference to the Germiyanids.</p><p>In the 17th century the Ottoman traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> described a square fortress with four gates and some fifty armed watchmen, with the main city outside it in 44 districts and 3,600 houses, 57 mosques, seven madrasahs, six baths and 17 dervish lodges. He wrote that everyone lived in vineyards, so that upper classes and ordinary people did not avoid one another. The city lived in peace for centuries without direct involvement in wars. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> the Greek Army reached Sarayköy, 20 kilometres to the north-west, but did not enter Denizli and was driven from the region by Mustafa Kemal Pasha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The urban area of Denizli forms parts of the districts of Merkezefendi and Pamukkale and had a population of 691 783 in 2024. Textile production and export have driven its economic development in recent decades. Visitors come to Pamukkale and Hierapolis, which are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>, and to the thermal spas at Karahayıt. Laodicea, founded by the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-ii-theos">Antiochus II</a> and named after his wife Laodice, was known for its woollen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloths; it later had one of the seven churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Denizli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An earthquake with an estimated magnitude of 7.8 to 8.0, centred on the southern shore of <b>Ladik</b> Lake, struck northern Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-17">17 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the later 13th century, as the Sultanate of Rum weakened, Turkmen frontier warriors took <b>Ladik</b>, near ancient Laodicea, and the Lycus valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/inancids">Inançids</a></li></ul></section>
